    "background_story": "Daniel was a charismatic professor of philosophy, known for his unorthodox teaching methods. He believed that the best way to convey complex ideas was through ostensive definition - showing, not telling. His lectures often involved elaborate demonstrations, using props and real-life examples to illustrate abstract concepts. One day, while teaching a lesson on free will, Daniel staged a mock debate between two students, using a cleverly designed maze to illustrate the concept of determinism. The students were initially confused, but as the demonstration unfolded, the penny dropped, and they grasped the idea with clarity. Daniel's ostensive approach made him a beloved figure on campus, and his students adored him for making the most obscure ideas accessible and engaging.",
